That's a great question, and it's one of the biggest conceptual differences in Databricks. The short answer is:

No. Connecting ADLS Gen2 does not change how your compute works. You are still using whatever compute you selected (Serverless or a Cluster).

Let's break it down.

Before connecting ADLS

Your architecture looked like this:

Notebook
    │
    ▼
Serverless Compute
    │
    ▼
Spark Driver
    │
    ▼
Spark Workers
    │
    ▼
Databricks-managed Storage (samples, managed tables, etc.)

The Spark Driver and Worker nodes already existed—they were just managed automatically by Databricks.

After connecting ADLS

Now the architecture becomes:

Notebook
    │
    ▼
Serverless Compute (or Cluster)
    │
    ▼
Spark Driver
    │
    ▼
Spark Workers
    │
    ▼
Unity Catalog
    │
    ▼
External Location
    │
    ▼
Storage Credential
    │
    ▼
Access Connector
    │
    ▼
ADLS Gen2 (raw container)

Notice that the compute layer didn't change. You only changed where the data is stored.

What was happening earlier?

When you ran:

df = spark.read.csv(...)

Databricks already started:

A Spark Driver
One or more Spark Workers

Even on Serverless Compute, these Spark components are created behind the scenes. You don't manage the VMs, but Spark is still there.

