Perfect. This is actually one of the most important concepts to understand if you're preparing for the Databricks Certified Data Engineer Associate exam or working with Databricks professionally.

The key idea is that storage and compute are independent. You can use the same ADLS storage with Serverless, Personal Compute, Shared Compute, or Job Compute.

Let's compare them.

1. Serverless Compute

This is what you've been using.

Notebook
     │
     ▼
Serverless Compute
     │
     ▼
Spark Driver
     │
     ▼
Spark Workers

Who manages it?

✅ Databricks

You don't know:

How many worker nodes exist
Which VM types are used
When they start
When they stop

Databricks automatically provisions the resources, runs your notebook, and scales them as needed.

Advantages
Starts quickly
No cluster management
Good for learning
Excellent for SQL and interactive notebooks
Disadvantages
Less control over Spark configuration
Some advanced Spark settings aren't customizable
Not ideal when you need specific hardware or networking
2. Personal Compute (Single User Cluster)
Notebook
     │
     ▼
Personal Compute
     │
     ▼
Driver
Worker 1
Worker 2

Who manages it?

You choose:
Driver VM size
Worker VM size
Number of workers
Autoscaling
Runtime version

Databricks still provisions the VMs, but you define the configuration.

Advantages
Full Spark control
Install custom libraries
Configure Spark properties
Better for development and debugging
3. Shared Compute

Multiple users share the same cluster.

User A Notebook ─┐
User B Notebook ─┼──► Shared Cluster
User C Notebook ─┘

Useful when a team wants to collaborate without creating separate clusters.

4. Job Compute

This is used for scheduled jobs.

Job starts
      │
      ▼
Cluster created
      │
      ▼
Run ETL
      │
      ▼
Cluster terminated

You don't pay for idle compute because the cluster exists only while the job runs.

