Example 1 – Serverless
df = spark.read.csv(
    "abfss://raw@datalakenishaan.dfs.core.windows.net/employees.csv"
)

Databricks automatically starts compute.

Example 2 – Personal Cluster

Exactly the same code:

df = spark.read.csv(
    "abfss://raw@datalakenishaan.dfs.core.windows.net/employees.csv"
)

The only difference is where the Spark Driver and Workers are running.

So why do companies create their own clusters?

Suppose you're processing 5 TB of data every night.

Serverless may not give you the exact environment you want.

You might want:

1 driver with 32 vCPUs
20 workers
Autoscaling between 20 and 100 workers
Photon enabled
Specific Spark configuration
Custom libraries

That's when you create your own cluster.

Example:

Driver:
Standard_E16ds_v5

Workers:
Standard_E16ds_v5

Autoscale:
20–100 workers

You control the infrastructure, while Databricks still manages provisioning and Spark.

When should you use each?
Scenario	Recommended Compute
Learning Databricks	✅ Serverless
Small notebooks	✅ Serverless
SQL Analytics	✅ Serverless SQL Warehouse
Team development	✅ Shared Compute
Personal development with custom Spark settings	✅ Personal Compute
Large ETL pipelines	✅ Job Compute
Heavy production workloads	✅ Job Compute or dedicated clusters
Think of it like driving
Serverless

It's like taking a taxi.

You say where you want to go.
Someone else drives.
You don't care what car it is.
Personal Cluster

It's your own car.

You choose the engine.
You choose the tires.
You decide how many passengers it carries.

More control, but also more responsibility.

